{"size":{"id":"a2b73db1-3a0f-4da2-b18c-164f65631128","word":"size","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"dimensions or magnitude of a thing","tags":["masculine"],"word":"tamanho"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"a specific set of dimensions for clothing","tags":["masculine"],"word":"tamanho"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The size of the building seemed to have increased since I was last there.","bold_text_offsets":[[4,8]],"type":"example","id":"6dde8b3d-8e6c-4c17-9622-84d88c459691"},{"text":"Ashley: Look at the size of that ship!\nKaidan: The Ascension. Flagship of the Citadel fleet.\nJoker: Well, size isn't everything.\nAshley: Why so touchy, Joker?\nJoker: I'm just saying you need firepower, too.","ref":"2008, BioWare, Mass Effect (Science Fiction), Redwood City: Electronic Arts, →ISBN, →OCLC, PC, scene: Normandy SR-1:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[20,24],[106,110]],"id":"cebb8289-4136-4017-92a1-0fdd42aec811"},{"text":"[The researchers] noticed many of their pieces of [plastic marine] debris sported surface pits around two microns across. Such pits are about the size of a bacterial cell. Closer examination showed that some of these pits did, indeed, contain bacteria, […].","ref":"2013 July 20, “Welcome to the plastisphere”, in The Economist, volume 408, number 8845:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[146,150]],"id":"ca20eb56-6e91-4b61-ae95-75c44eeca175"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 15th c.","references":[]}],"links":[["dimensions","dimensions"],["magnitude","magnitude"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"glosses":["The dimensions or magnitude of a thing; how big something is."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"3765b2b2-d26e-45b9-a814-e4aebc5f338f"},{"examples":[{"text":"I don't think we have the red one in your size.","bold_text_offsets":[[42,46]],"type":"example","id":"1b48a3f9-786b-4eb2-aaa5-08bf832446d7"},{"text":"What size are you? I mean, what size shoes do you take?","bold_text_offsets":[[5,9],[32,36]],"type":"example","id":"78908ba5-9da1-48a8-aab5-7e3033de1d8a"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 16th c.","references":[]}],"links":[["dimension","dimension"],["clothing","clothing"]],"glosses":["A specific set of dimensions for a manufactured article, especially clothing."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"b4e09084-cc9b-49b2-86fe-a2b39d8bea96"},{"topics":["graph-theory","mathematics","sciences"],"attestations":[{"date":"from 20th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["en:Graph theory"],"links":[["graph theory","graph theory"],["edges","edges"],["graph","graph"]],"raw_glosses":["(graph theory) The number of edges in a graph."],"glosses":["The number of edges in a graph."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"2ec38065-7523-4665-91dd-2351deaf61eb"},{"examples":[{"text":"men of a less size and quality**","ref":"1692, Roger L’Estrange, “ (please specify the fable number.) (please specify the name of the fable.)”, in Fables, of Æsop and Other Eminent Mythologists: […], London: […] R[ichard] Sare, […], →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[14,18]],"id":"955bb7a6-2f61-4fcf-a39a-e0298a3c979d"},{"text":"the middle or lower size of people","ref":"1720, Jonathan Swift, A Letter to a Young Clergyman:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[20,24]],"id":"0113c32e-d1d0-40a7-bf54-30473ab1d390"}],"categories":["English dated terms","English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"raw_glosses":["(figurative, dated) Degree of rank, ability, character, etc."],"glosses":["Degree of rank, ability, character, etc."],"tags":["countable","dated","figuratively","uncountable"],"id":"7aabeabf-39f6-44ad-92e9-9c43a9ee8cdd"},{"examples":[{"text":"Edward H[enry] Knight (1877), “Size”, in Knight’s American Mechanical Dictionary. […], volumes III (REA–ZYM), New York, N.Y.: Hurd and Houghton […], →OCLC